[![Netlify Status](https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/badges/dd389d80-cfcb-41ad-8856-93e439cf5ca2/deploy-status)](https://app.netlify.com/projects/fairsharing-benchmarks/deploys)
To setup:

Deploy on Netlify only

The Netlify deployment uses the existing ERB views and CSS to build static pages.
Ruby runs during the build only. A JavaScript Netlify Function replaces the
Sinatra POST endpoint in production and calls the same FAIR Champion service.
No separate Ruby server or Render deployment is needed. FAIR Champion remains
the external assessment service, as it is in the Sinatra version.

Current deployment: https://fairsharing-benchmarks.netlify.app
Netlify project: https://app.netlify.com/projects/fairsharing-benchmarks

The initial deployment was published directly from this working directory using
the CLI. It is not connected to Git yet, so pushes alone do not update it. Connect
FAIRsharing/BenchmarkFacade under the project's continuous deployment settings
to enable automatic builds after committing the Netlify files. To publish local
changes directly from the linked directory:

    npx netlify-cli deploy --build --prod

For a fresh Netlify project:

1. Commit these changes and push them to the Git repository you want to deploy.
2. In Netlify, add a project by importing that repository and select its branch.
3. Keep the settings from netlify.toml:
   Build command: bundle exec ruby scripts/build_netlify.rb
   Publish directory: dist
   Functions directory: netlify/functions
4. Deploy. Future pushes to the selected production branch rebuild the site and
   function together. Pulling changes updates your local checkout only.

Do not deploy dist by drag-and-drop: that omits the function required to run
assessments. Import the Git repository or deploy with the Netlify CLI instead.

Local Netlify preview (after the Ruby setup below, with Node.js 24 on PATH):

    bundle exec ruby scripts/build_netlify.rb
    npx netlify-cli dev

Open the address printed by Netlify Dev. It serves the static pages and emulates
the function. After editing a view, configuration, CSS or JavaScript, rebuild
the static pages before previewing the change. The original Sinatra development
server remains available with the instructions below.

Both deployments read config/benchmarks.json. Add or edit benchmark definitions
there, then rebuild. Netlify accepts HTTP(S) GUIDs up to 2048 characters and
returns the complete Champion response without filtering conditions or tests.
The API allows 55 seconds for Champion, leaving time to return a readable timeout
error within Netlify's 60-second synchronous function limit. Assessments that
regularly exceed this need a background function with result storage and polling.
See https://docs.netlify.com/build/functions/overview/ and
https://docs.netlify.com/build/functions/background-functions/.

Local Sinatra setup

1. install RVM.
2. cd to the app's directory, RVM should detect the necessary gemset.
3. `gem install bundler` and then `bundle install`.
4. `bundle exec rackup -E development -o 127.0.0.1 -p 4567`

Open http://127.0.0.1:4567 in your browser. Stop the server with Ctrl+C.

macOS: Ruby installation fails while compiling OpenSSL

If the project's Ruby fails to compile and make.log mentions OpenSSL 4 headers or
"incomplete definition of type", explicitly select Homebrew's OpenSSL 3:

    brew install openssl@3 libyaml readline zlib
    source "$HOME/.rvm/scripts/rvm"
    rvm install "$(cat .ruby-version)" --with-openssl-dir="$(brew --prefix openssl@3)" --with-opt-dir="$(brew --prefix openssl@3):$(brew --prefix libyaml):$(brew --prefix readline):$(brew --prefix zlib)" --autolibs=read-only
    rvm use "$(cat .ruby-version)@$(cat .ruby-gemset)" --create
    gem install bundler -v 4.0.24 --no-document
    bundle install
    bundle exec rackup -E development -o 127.0.0.1 -p 4567

Run these commands from the app's directory. Use openssl@3 explicitly: the
unversioned Homebrew openssl formula may point to OpenSSL 4. The Bundler version
above matches Gemfile.lock.

To run the tests and generate local coverage:

1. Run `bundle exec rake test`.
2. Open `coverage/index.html` in a browser to view the coverage report.

The test task runs Minitest and the JavaScript tests. JavaScript tests use Node.js's
built-in test runner and require no npm packages. Ensure a recent Node.js version
(22 or later) is on PATH inside the test environment. If `node` is unavailable,
only the JavaScript tests are skipped, with a warning. JavaScript test failures
fail the task when Node.js is available.

Run `bundle exec rake test:ruby` or `bundle exec rake test:javascript` to run either
suite separately. The SimpleCov report covers Ruby code only.
